# Regulatory and Political Brief — Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals

**Analyst lens:** federal, state, and local regulatory and political forces that bind MWAA's decision to adopt a common quiet-by-design terminal standard across IAD and DCA. This brief tells the Strategist what can and cannot be moved, and on what realistic timeframe.

## Bottom line up top

A quiet-by-design standard at MWAA is not primarily an acoustics question. It is a compliance-stack question layered on a political stack that starts at the White House. Six federal instruments — NFPA 72 (adopted via Virginia and DC building codes), 49 CFR 27.71, 14 CFR 382.53, 14 CFR Part 139.319, DOT Title VI/LEP guidance, and AIP/BIL grant assurances — set the floor. None of them can be relaxed by MWAA. The binding political facts: MWAA operates under a DOT federal lease; the July 2026 IAD $20B+ transformation was co-branded with the White House, DOT, and United; and the 15-year Airline Use and Lease Agreement effective January 1, 2025 gives signatory carriers majority-in-interest leverage over cost-recovery changes through December 31, 2039. The January 29, 2025 DCA midair collision and the NTSB's February 2026 final report have raised the political price of any decision that could be read as reducing emergency communication capacity. A standard framed as "reducing announcements" will lose. A standard framed as "specifying intelligibility, visual redundancy, and zoning before consultants harden designs on Concourse C/D and DCA Terminal 1" will pass, because it lives inside the Design Manual apparatus MWAA already uses to bind consultants, tenants, and airlines. The window is now: Concourse E opens fall 2026, and the design gates for the next generation of IAD and DCA projects fall inside the 2026–2028 bond issuance window.

## Key findings

- **NFPA 72 sets the floor and it is not negotiable.** NFPA 72 Chapter 24 requires emergency voice communication systems to achieve an average Speech Transmission Index of at least 0.50 (0.70 CIS) with a 0.45 (0.65 CIS) minimum in 90% of acoustically distinguishable spaces. Virginia and DC adopt NFPA 72 by reference through their building codes; MWAA cannot design below the floor. "Quiet by design" must be written as *layered on top of* NFPA 72, not as an alternative to it. [Source: https://www.suppressionsystems.com/voice-intelligibility-in-mass-notification-systems/]
- **49 CFR 27.71 requires visual paging as a condition of federal money.** DOT's Section 504 rule for airport facilities receiving federal financial assistance requires that PA messages be displayed on video monitors so deaf and hard-of-hearing passengers do not miss information available to others. This is not aspirational; it is a grant-eligibility condition. Any quiet-by-design standard must specify the visual channel as mandatory, not "recommended." [Source: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-49/subtitle-A/part-27/subpart-B/section-27.71]
- **14 CFR 382.53 pushes the same obligation onto the airlines.** The Air Carrier Access Act rule requires carriers to give passengers with vision or hearing impairment "the same information" made generally available to other passengers about gate assignments, delays, cancellations, boarding, baggage, and schedule changes. That means gate-podium audio at DCA and IAD lives inside the standard's scope whether MWAA installs the equipment or not. Any tenant carve-out for airline audio is legally dubious and operationally counterproductive. [Source: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-II/subchapter-D/part-382/subpart-D/section-382.53]
- **Part 139.319 makes emergency-communication capacity a certification issue, post-1/29/25.** FAA-certificated airports must maintain an Airport Emergency Plan and a communication system capable of alerting emergency response personnel. The NTSB February 2026 final report on the January 29, 2025 DCA midair collision found multiple failures across FAA and ATC and drove FAA to remove Helicopter Route 4 charting between Hains Point and the Woodrow Wilson Bridge. DCA's emergency-communication capacity is now on a hotter regulatory watch. A standard that even appears to reduce announcement redundancy at DCA fails politically before it fails technically. [Source: https://www.faa.gov/airports/airport_safety/certalerts/part_139_certalert_24_09]
- **Title VI/LEP obligations bind harder at IAD than most U.S. hubs.** DOT's 2001 and 2005 LEP guidance requires federal-assistance recipients to take "reasonable steps" to ensure limited-English-proficient passengers have meaningful access. IAD's 36%+ international share (approximately 10.53 million international passengers in 2025 per the context packet) puts MWAA in the highest-exposure tier for Title VI. Removing audio redundancy without a parallel visual, multilingual, or staffed channel is a Title VI risk, not a passenger-experience preference. [Source: https://www.transportation.gov/civil-rights/civil-rights-awareness-enforcement/title-vi-executive-order-13166]
- **AIP grant assurances turn FAA Advisory Circulars into binding standards.** Federal money — including BIL Airport Terminal Program grants and AIP formula and discretionary awards — attaches Grant Assurance 34 (Policies, Standards, and Specifications), which obliges sponsors to conform to FAA-approved AC series (150/5360-13B for terminal planning; 150/5360-14A for access to airports by individuals with disabilities). A MWAA quiet-by-design standard that adopts or references the relevant ACs inherits federal enforceability at no additional political cost. One that quietly diverges from them exposes future grant draws to clawback and audit risk. [Source: https://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/media/Advisory_Circular/150-5360-14A.pdf]
- **The FAA Reauthorization Act of 2024 runs through FY2028 and does not open the accessibility playing field.** Public Law 118-63, signed May 16, 2024, created a Passenger Experience Advisory Committee (Section 517) and reinforced ACAA obligations in Sections 541–551. Congress is moving the accessibility ratchet one direction only. Any standard MWAA writes should assume the FY2028 reauthorization will tighten, not loosen, disability and LEP obligations. [Source: https://www.congress.gov/118/plaws/publ63/PLAW-118publ63.pdf]
- **The Airline Use and Lease Agreement is the political constraint the accountants underweight.** The 15-year agreement that took effect January 1, 2025 governs cost recovery through December 31, 2039. Majority-in-interest and cost-recovery mechanics were not verified in Stage-0 primary documents, but the industry convention is that signatory carriers can veto or delay cost-recoverable capital that raises cost per enplanement. A standard adopted as an Office of Engineering Design Manual amendment — pre-design, pre-scope — is much harder for United (at IAD) or American (at DCA) to challenge than a change order priced against a hardened concourse design.

## Evidence: what binds, and what to write into the standard

### Compliance floor (unchangeable on any project timeframe)

- **NFPA 72 Chapter 24 STI.** Adopt STI ≥ 0.50 as the mandatory design target for all voice-alarm zones and require STI verification as a commissioning deliverable. ACRP 175 notes STI 0.50 is challenging above 24-foot ceilings — a direct constraint for the Saarinen Main Terminal and the DCA Terminal 2 vault. [Source: https://crp.trb.org/acrp0715/wp-content/themes/acrp-child/documents/201/original/acrp_r175.pdf]
- **49 CFR 27.71 visual paging.** Specify latency (visual within a defined window of audio), coverage (every seat in every hold room within sight of a display), and content parity (visual message = audio message, not truncated). [Source: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-49/subtitle-A/part-27/subpart-B/section-27.71]
- **14 CFR 382.53 airline messaging.** Pulls airline gate-podium and club audio into scope. A tenant carve-out leaves MWAA carrying the compliance risk while airlines keep operational discretion — a bad trade. [Source: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-II/subchapter-D/part-382/subpart-D/section-382.53]
- **14 CFR Part 139.319 emergency communication.** Post-1/29/25, any DCA standard that reads as reducing communication redundancy is politically radioactive; MWAA institutional memory will not tolerate it either. [Source: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-I/subchapter-G/part-139]
- **DOT Title VI / LEP.** Specify multilingual visual as the primary LEP channel and staffed language assistance as backup — the defensible "reasonable steps" posture without an unwinnable audio-translation matrix. [Source: https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2005/12/14/05-23972/policy-guidance-concerning-recipients-responsibilities-to-limited-english-proficient-lep-persons]

### Federal grant lever (movable but expensive to move)

Adopting FAA AC 150/5360-14A and AC 150/5360-13B by reference inside the MWAA standard is close to free politically; diverging from them costs grant flexibility on every BIL Airport Terminal Program and AIP discretionary award through the FY2028 authorization horizon. BIL money already flowing to Concourse East (~$898.2M with ~$235.8M grant-funded per the context packet) is inside the assurance perimeter, and every future draw against Concourse C/D or DCA Terminal 1 will carry the same assurances. Write the standard to *use* Assurance 34 as uniform enforcement, not to work around it. [Source: https://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/media/Advisory_Circular/150-5360-14A.pdf]

### Political stack (immovable on project timeframes)

- **DOT federal lease + White House altitude.** The July 29, 2026 IAD announcement staged the President, Transportation Secretary, United, and MWAA on one stage. Write the standard to be presentable there — accessibility-forward, emergency-communication-preserving, measurable — not as a passenger-experience nicety.
- **Bi-state 17-member Board.** VA 7, DC 4, MD 3, President 3. Standards changes affecting cost, airline recovery, or federal grant eligibility require Board action. Adopt inside the current officer term to reduce re-litigation risk.
- **Use and Lease Agreement (Jan 1, 2025 – Dec 31, 2039).** MII thresholds not verified in Stage-0 primary sources and should not be assumed. What is knowable: airline consent is the practical constraint on cost-recoverable spending for 14 more years. A design-manual standard adopted before consultant scopes sits outside the MII conversation; adopted after, it lands inside it as a change-order fight.
- **Post-1/29/25 DCA context.** February 2026 NTSB final report found FAA/ATC/military failures; FAA on March 14, 2025 removed Helicopter Route 4 charting between Hains Point and the Woodrow Wilson Bridge. Congressional interest in DCA safety is at a multi-decade high. Wrong moment for anything readable as "fewer DCA announcements."
- **DCA slot control and perimeter rule.** FAA Reauth 2024 added 10 daily beyond-perimeter exemptions (Alaska/American/Delta/Southwest/United), awarded December 2024. Everything else stays. Will not shift inside the standards window.

### Not actually binding (background, not constraint)

- **Community noise coalitions** are aircraft-noise coalitions. Part 150 studies address the noise-exposure map, not terminal-interior acoustics. Do not confuse the constituencies.
- **State aviation agencies** matter for GA and reliever grant matching, not large-hub terminal design. Virginia and DC building codes matter (via NFPA adoption); state aviation departments do not.
- **PFC cap** ($4.50 since 2001, four failed raise attempts since 2017) shapes what MWAA can afford, not what MWAA can specify.

## Case studies where regulation or politics determined the outcome

- **Post-9/11 checkpoint reconfiguration.** TSA's checkpoint design authority overrode airport commercial preferences at essentially every U.S. hub. Airports that hardened terminal designs before TSA standards stabilized paid for it twice. The lesson for a quiet-by-design standard: write it before consultants scope Concourse C/D and DCA Terminal 1, or pay for the change orders.
- **SFO Quiet Airport program.** SFO reports removed more than 90 minutes of daily announcement time (measurement methodology not verified in Stage-0 primary sources per the context packet). SFO's political environment — a single-authority airport with a strong sustainability mandate and no bi-state board — is not MWAA's. Direct transfer of the SFO operating model to DCA, in particular, runs into the post-1/29/25 political reality that SFO does not face.
- **1/29/25 DCA collision → March 14, 2025 Helicopter Route 4 removal.** A single event moved FAA charting policy in six weeks. The inverse is instructive: the same political salience that allowed FAA to act fast on helicopter routes will punish any airport authority that appears to be reducing DCA safety communications.

